{"kind":"grammar","lang":"en","entities":{"n2-140":{"renderings":[{"id":"03783d85b112949b","role":"prose","field":"shortExplanation","source":{"source":"hanabira","ref":"n2-140"},"match":"id","status":"imported","value":"Not necessarily true that if A, then B."},{"id":"093a7fbbfddda913","role":"prose","field":"longExplanation","source":{"source":"hanabira","ref":"n2-140"},"match":"id","status":"imported","value":"The ～ば～というものでもない grammar point is used to express that something is not necessarily true even if a certain condition is met. In other words, if A happens, it doesn't always mean B will happen."},{"id":"b24379fa3982d16f","role":"prose","field":"formation","source":{"source":"hanabira","ref":"n2-140"},"match":"id","status":"imported","value":"Verb-ば form + というものでもない"},{"id":"04acb9a8fec952db","role":"prose","field":"usageNote","source":{"source":"gokan","ref":"n2-140"},"match":"id","status":"reviewed","value":"ば～というものでもない: doing X does not automatically mean Y."},{"id":"26390be97fdc4034","role":"label","field":"family","source":{"source":"gokan","ref":"not-necessarily"},"match":"id","status":"reviewed","value":"Not Necessarily (An Exception Exists)","of":{"kind":"family","id":"not-necessarily"}}]},"n2-140#f60855e4fcf4":{"renderings":[{"id":"b9e51e2add1348a2","role":"translation","source":{"source":"hanabira","ref":"n2-140"},"match":"id","status":"imported","value":"Just because you can speak English doesn’t mean you’ll succeed."}]},"n2-140#fe5064d75a42":{"renderings":[{"id":"76ece0ff1e4fa0f3","role":"translation","source":{"source":"hanabira","ref":"n2-140"},"match":"id","status":"imported","value":"Just because someone is beautiful on the outside, it doesn’t mean they’re beautiful on the inside."}]},"n2-140#9a5b1ce10f34":{"renderings":[{"id":"c13f0c87a657fa00","role":"translation","source":{"source":"hanabira","ref":"n2-140"},"match":"id","status":"imported","value":"Getting into a famous university doesn’t guarantee a secure future."}]}}}